schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Mempool (transaction waiting room)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    The pool of unconfirmed transactions a blockchain node holds after a
    transaction is broadcast but before it is included in a block; higher
    fees generally earn priority inclusion in the next block.
  source_traditions:
    - bitcoin/ethereum node-software terminology
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Introduced in how-transactions-work.md as step 3 ("Broadcasting") of the
    seven-step transaction journey, framed as a "waiting room" for
    not-yet-confirmed transactions.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - block
    - gas-fee-priority
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      Being in the mempool is not the same as being confirmed — a
      transaction can sit in the mempool indefinitely (or be dropped) if fees
      are too low relative to network demand.
  tensions:
    - >-
      "Higher fees = priority seating" framing simplifies real fee-market
      dynamics (fee estimation, replace-by-fee, congestion spikes).
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    The "bus with limited seats" analogy is a helpful beginner mental model
    but does not capture that mempool contents differ across nodes (each
    node's mempool is a local, not globally synchronized, view).
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/how-transactions-work.md
    transformation: synthesized
    authorship: ai-assisted
